Why this package uses `3.0 (native)` (see `debian/source/format`).

This project is its own upstream with a single version stream:
releases are cut from this repository (`v*` tags drive
`.github/workflows/release.yml`), and the `.deb` is built directly
from the tagged tree in CI (`.github/workflows/build-packages.yml`
stamps `debian/changelog` at release time). That is why `native` is
deliberate.

Separately, `3.0 (quilt)` would change the source-package layout to
`.orig.tar.*` plus `.debian.tar.*` archives. dpkg-source can generate
the `.orig` from the source directory (no pre-existing upstream
tarball needed), but CI would then have to produce and carry those
archives on every source build, for no change in what actually ships.
`native` avoids that overhead; revisit only if a genuinely separate
upstream release artifact exists one day.
